stance is hydrogen cyanide so it's  the one that can cause death just think  of the skull and crossbones is a nice  way of remembering that it causes death  okay next up this exclamation mark this  sign here means harmful okay so it can  basically cause irritation reddening or  blistering of the skin so if you spill  this chemical onto your hands these are  the different problems that it could  potentially cause an example of this  being bleach so if you are spill bleach  on your hands it could cause irritation  reddening and could also cause blisters  right next up we have this one the one  that looks like a fire just simply means  highly flammable now a highly flammable  substance is one that catches fire  easily so you want to keep this one way  away from an open flame because it can  catch fire really really readily really  easily an example would be petrol now  people often get these two symbols  confused highly flammable and oxidizing  the way that I remember which ones which  is if you have a look at oxidizing that  there that circle looks like an O bow  for oxidizing so that's the way that I  tell the difference between them highly  flammable means that substance itself  catches fire easily oxidizing means it  just produces and provides oxygen into  reaction which therefore causes more  burning or more combustion to occur this  one here is corrosive so what corrosive  means  is it destroys materials now as you can  see from the diagram this could be  materials as in material substances or  biological substances for example skin  so concentrated sulfuric acid  concentrated sulfuric acid is an example  of this if you get it on your skin it  can burn right through it could also  damage different materials as well  now what before we use any of these  substances in science we need to do  something called a risk assessment in a  risk assessment we look at a particular  substance to try and think about how we  can make sure we're not going to get  hurt when we use it so for example let's  imagine I was using this substance here  someone has it is soft uric acid that's  the thing which could harm me so the  hazard is the thing which could harm me  what harm can it cause so what it could  do to me is it could damage my skin and  my eyes if I'm not careful so the whole  point of risk assessment is to think  about what am I going to do to keep  myself safe the next thing I need to  think about is how likely is it that I  was going to get yourself uric acid on  my skin or in my eyes well I'd say  there's a kind of medium chance of this  happening it's quite easy to to have a  small spillage of a substance onto your  hands so what could I do in order to  help now this part here is the most  important part as you can see there's a  nice way of working way through risk  assessment because each stage begins  with age hazard harm how likely help so  this final stage is the really important  one considering the damage that could be  caused the harm that could be caused  from the hazard what can I do to make  sure I don't get harmed by this so what  I could do just prevent the damage of  the skin I could wear gloves to prevent  damage of my eyes I could wear goggles  and also I've got to try and use a low  concentration as possible because  obviously the lower the concentration  the less corrosive that this substance  is going to be so that is these are the  different hazards that we need to know  and this is how to complete a risk  assessment thank you very much
 

